 universe means that if her kids get sick  or say have trouble getting into a top  tier college she can travel around the  multiverse and find novel medicines or a  really great s.a.t tutor and again kill  a version of herself she tries to  persuade strange to go along with her  plan but he refuses and rallies all of  comtaj to protect america wanda it seems  can outpower just about any living being  but she can't magic her way into  motherhood and then there's doctor  strange who after arguably saving the  world still has to sit by and watch the  love of his life christine marry some  other guy she even reminds him that you  always have to be the one holding the  knife that is to say he is a total  control freak the film opens with an  alternate strange in america running  from a demon having lost their lead  strange steals america's powers which he  knows will kill her rather than yielding  them to a monstrous force he's down to  take control of the situation even if it  means doing a murder though luckily the  demon murks him before he murks america  and throughout the film strange tries to  control everything he desperately tries  to stop wanda's antics axes america's  caretaker acquires the book of vashanti  and so on like wanda he even tries out  the dangerous new sport of dreamwalking  to control of all things his very own  decaying corpse we've got zombies in the  mcu folks but after a magic induced  massacre strange is faced with the  frightening reality that he might not be  able to control wanda's fury or save  america so here we have an interesting  miriam effect where our protagonist and  antagonist actually have the exact same  problem and in case you missed it it's  neatly summed up in the trailer by  sorcerer supreme wong you can't control  everything strange in contrast we have  america who maintains that she cannot  control her power to travel the  multiverse it only manifests when she's  scared unlike strange and wanda she's  keenly aware that many things are out of  her control so how can we make sense of  strange and wanda's shared desire for  control well with the help of our  favorite danish walking enthusiast soren  kierkegaard and guys i get it i talk  about him all the time but i went into  an unpayable amount of grad school debt  to learn about him so i gotta milk this  for all it's worth now in kierkegaard's  book repetition he tells a comedic tale  of a young man who was trying to  recreate or repeat a nice trip he took  to berlin because it made him so happy  at the time he goes back and stays at  the same place visits the same theater  hangs at the same coffee shop still the  initial magic is gone and none of these  experiences are as meaningful as they  were the first time and this isn't too  dissimilar from wanda's desire to  recreate or repeat the experience she  had as a mother though in fairness  kierkegaard's character wasn't trying to  kill a hip teen with superpowers one of  the ways that wanda attempts to do this  is to use dreamwalking to literally  occupy the body of another wanda in the  multiverse one who is calmly enjoying  her suburban life with her two boys but  during this experience our wanda seems  oddly out of sync and unable to connect  with this other version of herself and  for that matter with the children she  seemingly loves so much kierkegaard  describes something similar in  repetition eventually after doing  everything possible to repeat his  beloved trip the young man in berlin  realizes that there really isn't such a  thing as literal repetition and in  attempting to repeat the experience a  contradiction arises the conflict  between his ideal for how the trip would  feel and what it actually feels like in  reality the attempted repetition left  him with this feeling of contradiction  and or impossibility and we see this  when scarlet witch wanda literally comes  face to face with suburban mom wanda and  her kids everything she's done has been  motivated by wanting to once again  experience the love she had for her boys  when she finally sees them again they  are absolutely [ __ ] terrified of her  in this moment she experiences a  shattering disconnect between the ideal  of once again being a mother and the  reality of these kids thinking she's  super scary and that's the comic and in  this case tragic irony of repetition for  kierkegaard in trying to repeat  experiences we ultimately confront the  absolute impossibility of repetition put  differently and trying to control our  future we realize that total control is  impossible kierkegaard wrote in his  journals that life can only be  understood backwards but it must be  lived forward meaning that we should  learn from our past experiences instead  of trying to uncritically repeat them  ultimately wanda stops trying to control  the world around her and instead  destroys the darkhold so that nobody  might ever be tempted by its evils again  our guy dr strange has a similar  realization in the film though he's not  trying to recreate anything per se he  still wants things to go his way this  has been his move throughout his  appearances in the mcu he's kind of a  cocky prick and he's not the only one  throughout the film he encounters other  strangers from across the multiverse  whose same hubristic desire for control  led to their downfall what eventually  sets 616 strange apart is that he's able  to sacrifice this desire for control  namely over whether his lived experience  matches up with his ideal vision for  things it might also help that 616  strange in 838 christine got flirty as  hell he's eventually able to cede  control to america when he realizes he  can't defeat wanda and for once he needs  to give the proverbial knife to someone  else meanwhile america has the opposite  experience initially she feels like she  has no control over her powers her  reality and even whether she'll ever see  her moms again by the end she realizes  that even if she can't control what's  happening around her she can learn to  control herself and her powers this is  pretty meaningful existentialists  believe that while we have no control  over the conditions in which we exist we  do have responsibility for ourselves and  our own actions for better or worse this  is why for jean-paul sartre  responsibility is a direct effect of our  existential freedom this tension between  control and lack thereof between wanting  to live in the ideal world and accepting  that we live in the real messy complex  world is nothing new it's been essential  to western philosophy ever since some  greeks and ancient birkenstocks invented  it descartes thought that math with a  side of god made existence into a nice  rational structure where everything  makes sense and you know you exist  because you're thinking about existing  this made reality something that was  understandable with a level of  mathematical certainty david hume on the  other hand thought that this was dumb  and all of our knowledge is based on  lived experience what we see is more  important than what we think kant sort  of mix these two together to argue that  reality does have a formal structure and  while we can't see the causes for  everything we can know some limited  stuff about how the world works for  example we can know some of the  fundamental principles of things like  morality and truth without seeing inside  the mind of god but my debt lord  kierkegaard on the other hand was  interested in how all these attempts at  knowledge affect us at the individual  level in our actual existence and as he  saw it this fundamental tension between  the ideal world and the real world or  ideality in actuality can lead to  despair like the young man in berlin or  the scarlet witch who wants to be a mom  we all have experienced a contradiction  between how we want our world to be and  how our world actually is and as strange  wanda and america learn kierkegaard  thinks the way out of this despair is  making decisions and these decisions  always involve an element of risk or  faith because there is no way of being  certain that your idea is going to be  actualized doctor strange must decide to  give control to america and not insist  on holding the knife or the spooky book  of evil spells wanda needs to decide  that her plan is evil and that to start  writing her wrongs she needs to destroy  the darkhold and seemingly herself  meanwhile america decides that she's  capable of controlling her power and  this decision is accompanied by the  faith that she'll be able to do so it  should also be no surprise that  kierkegaard famously emphasized the  importance of the leap of faith the  paradox of all of this is that there is  power in giving up control and that the  most heroic acts in the film are  precisely those decisions to cede power  for the sake of doing what's right we  can't force our lives to look like the  past but we can decide to live  differently in the future the drive for  repetition and control can be a prison  and the way out is deciding to give up  control and have faith that the future  can still work out even if you can't be  certain about it but what do you guys  think can doctor strange in the  multiverse of madness teach us a thing  or two about accepting that we're not in  control of everything or should we all  search for a secret book of evil magic  so we can make our crush like us let us  know what you think in the comments big  thanks to our patrons for all your  support and if you haven't checked out  our patreon page in a while do it we're  making lots of great new content and  remember to check out our new live  stream wisecrack live every thursday  right here on youtube hit the subscribe  button like you're punching a hole into  the universe where tacos are free and  the big bang theory never made it past  its pilot episode and don't forget to  ring that bell and as always thanks for  watching later  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

